          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Family and Succession</strong><br>\nTheodosius was married twice and had several children, including two sons who would succeed him: Arcadius and Honorius. He is known for promoting his sons to augusti, ensuring that upon his death, they would rule the Eastern and Western Empires respectively, a division that lasted until the fall of the Western Roman Empire.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nTheodosius died on January 17, 395 AD, leaving a divided empire to his sons. His death marked the permanent division of the Roman Empire into Eastern and Western halves. His policies and actions had long-lasting effects on European history, particularly his enforcement of Christianity as the empire's official religion.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Impact on Christianity</strong><br>\nTheodosius's reign had a profound impact on the spread and dominance of Christianity throughout Europe. By making Christianity the state religion and suppressing pagan practices, he shaped the religious landscape of the empire. This decision influenced the course of Western civilization by entwining the church with the state's structure.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
